{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.112.132159","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":5,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"23564668","source":"MED","pmid":"23564668","doi":"10.1161/circulationaha.112.132159","title":"NADPH oxidase 1 plays a key role in diabetes mellitus-accelerated atherosclerosis.","authorString":"Gray SP, Di Marco E, Okabe J, Szyndralewiez C, Heitz F, Montezano AC, de Haan JB, Koulis C, El-Osta A, El-Osta A, Andrews KL, Chin-Dusting JP, Touyz RM, Wingler K, Cooper ME, Schmidt HH, Jandeleit-Dahm KA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gray SP","lastName":"Gray","firstName":"Stephen P","initials":"SP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Diabetic Complications Division, Baker IDI Heart & Diabetes Institute, Melbourne, VIctoria 8008, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Di Marco E","lastName":"Di Marco","firstName":"Elyse","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Okabe J","lastName":"Okabe","firstName":"Jun","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7196-0522"}},{"fullName":"Szyndralewiez C","lastName":"Szyndralewiez","firstName":"Cedric","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Heitz F","lastName":"Heitz","firstName":"Freddy","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Montezano AC","lastName":"Montezano","firstName":"Augusto C","initials":"AC"},{"fullName":"de Haan JB","lastName":"de Haan","firstName":"Judy B","initials":"JB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2749-7833"}},{"fullName":"Koulis C","lastName":"Koulis","firstName":"Christine","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"El-Osta A","lastName":"El-Osta","firstName":"Assam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7968-7375"}},{"fullName":"El-Osta A","lastName":"El-Osta","firstName":"Assam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2969-9137"}},{"fullName":"Andrews KL","lastName":"Andrews","firstName":"Karen L","initials":"KL"},{"fullName":"Chin-Dusting JP","lastName":"Chin-Dusting","firstName":"Jaye P F","initials":"JP"},{"fullName":"Touyz RM","lastName":"Touyz","firstName":"Rhian M","initials":"RM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0670-0887"}},{"fullName":"Wingler K","lastName":"Wingler","firstName":"Kirstin","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Cooper ME","lastName":"Cooper","firstName":"Mark E","initials":"ME","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7785-2488"}},{"fullName":"Schmidt HH","lastName":"Schmidt","firstName":"Harald H H W","initials":"HH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0419-5549"}},{"fullName":"Jandeleit-Dahm KA","lastName":"Jandeleit-Dahm","firstName":"Karin A","initials":"KA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2192-5822"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7785-2488"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7968-7375"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2749-7833"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3651-7621"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7196-0522"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0419-5549"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0670-0887"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2192-5822"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2969-9137"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"18","volume":"127","journalIssueId":2036707,"dateOfPublication":"2013 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2013,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Circulation","title":"Circulation","essn":"1524-4539","issn":"0009-7322","isoabbreviation":"Circulation","nlmid":"0147763"},"printPublicationDate":"2013-05-01"},"pubYear":"2013","pageInfo":"1888-1902","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>In diabetes mellitus, vascular complications such as atherosclerosis are a major cause of death. The key underlying pathomechanisms are unclear. However, hyperglycemic oxidative stress derived from NADPH oxidase (Nox), the only known dedicated enzyme to generate reactive oxygen species appears to play a role. Here we identify the Nox1 isoform as playing a key and pharmacologically targetable role in the accelerated development of diabetic atherosclerosis.<h4>Methods and results</h4>Human aortic endothelial cells exposed to hyperglycemic conditions showed increased expression of Nox1, oxidative stress, and proinflammatory markers in a Nox1-siRNA reversible manner. Similarly, the specific Nox inhibitor, GKT137831, prevented oxidative stress in response to hyperglycemia in human aortic endothelial cells. To examine these observations in vivo, we investigated the role of Nox1 on plaque development in apolipoprotein E-deficient mice 10 weeks after induction of diabetes mellitus. Deletion of Nox1, but not Nox4, had a profound antiatherosclerotic effect correlating with reduced reactive oxygen species formation, attenuation of chemokine expression, vascular adhesion of leukocytes, macrophage infiltration, and reduced expression of proinflammatory and profibrotic markers. Similarly, treatment of diabetic apolipoprotein E-deficient mice with GKT137831 attenuated atherosclerosis development.<h4>Conclusions</h4>These studies identify a major pathological role for Nox1 and suggest that Nox1-dependent oxidative stress is a promising target for diabetic vasculopathies, including atherosclerosis.","affiliation":"Diabetic Complications Division, Baker IDI Heart & Diabetes Institute, Melbourne, VIctoria 8008, Australia.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cells, Cultured"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endothelial Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descri